WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.160
You know, usually when we talk about a game or really any kind of app you might interact with on your phone, there's this foundational expectation of boundaries.

00:09.360 --> 00:10.480
Right, like a physical limit.

00:10.740 --> 00:13.480
Exactly. It's a lot like stepping onto a basketball court.

00:13.880 --> 00:19.600
You lace up your shoes, you walk onto the hardwood, you play the game, and eventually the buzzer sounds.

00:19.840 --> 00:20.520
And then it's over.

00:20.780 --> 00:25.060
Right. Win or lose, you walk off the court, and the game is definitively over.

00:25.600 --> 00:26.880
It's bounded. It's contained.

00:26.880 --> 00:30.440
Yeah, which is a really important psychological boundary for us.

00:30.600 --> 00:40.220
I mean, you might be exhausted, right? You might be frustrated that you missed a shot, but your obligation to that activity is entirely finished until you decide to walk back onto the court.

00:40.440 --> 00:44.920
And that physical and temporal boundary is incredibly comforting to the human brain.

00:45.660 --> 00:50.120
We naturally crave knowing that our obligations have a definitive stopping point.

00:50.280 --> 00:51.000
Oh, absolutely.

00:51.000 --> 00:59.700
When we step off the court, or when we turn off a screen, our social and functional responsibilities to that specific activity just cease to exist.

01:00.020 --> 01:08.440
But then you look at modern, persistent digital environments, and you realize that for millions of people, that buzzer never actually sounds.

01:08.640 --> 01:10.560
Never. The clock just keeps running.

01:10.840 --> 01:19.700
We are currently navigating a digital landscape that is, like, structurally engineered, so that stepping away feels entirely impossible without letting someone down.

01:19.700 --> 01:21.880
It's a completely different paradigm.

01:21.880 --> 01:25.500
It really is. I mean, have you ever engaged with a piece of software?

01:25.940 --> 01:37.200
Maybe it was a game. Maybe it was a collaborative work tool where the mere thought of just turning it off and going to sleep gave you a spike of genuine visceral anxiety.

01:37.520 --> 01:39.800
Oh, I think everyone listening right now is nodding their head.

01:39.880 --> 01:46.880
Right. And not because you were having so much fun you couldn't put it down, but because you felt like you were abandoning your team in a foxhole.

01:46.880 --> 01:59.560
Yeah. It is a profoundly common, modern experience, yet we rarely possess the vocabulary to explain exactly why a configuration of code can manufacture that specific type of dread.

01:59.700 --> 02:02.040
We really don't. We just sort of blame ourselves.

02:02.400 --> 02:10.880
Exactly. We tend to internalize it as a personal failing, right? A lack of willpower. We don't recognize it as a deliberate architectural choice.

02:10.960 --> 02:15.040
And giving you that vocabulary is exactly what we're doing today on this deep dive.

02:15.040 --> 02:16.120
It's an important one.

02:16.400 --> 02:25.200
It is. We are unpacking an incredibly eye-opening essay published in August 2026 by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

02:25.560 --> 02:26.780
The title alone is great.

02:26.880 --> 02:32.640
Yeah. The paper is titled, Unfinishable Games, Real-Time Structure as a Distinct Mechanism of Extraction.

02:33.080 --> 02:38.740
And, I mean, this research fundamentally shifts the paradigm of how we view digital exploitation.

02:38.960 --> 02:40.300
It completely flips the script.

02:40.300 --> 02:46.120
Because when you hear terms like video game addiction, the conversation almost universally defaults to money, right?

02:46.180 --> 02:47.820
It's all about monetization schemes.

02:48.360 --> 02:52.280
That financial angle has completely dominated the discourse for a decade.

02:52.960 --> 03:00.420
I mean, lawmakers, consumer protection groups, psychologists, they've focused almost all of their regulatory fire on one specific mechanism.

03:00.560 --> 03:01.280
The loot box.

03:01.560 --> 03:02.700
Right. The loot box.

03:02.700 --> 03:14.480
And if we want to understand the new hidden architectural threat this paper exposes, we first have to understand the massive shadow cast by that monetization debate.

03:14.480 --> 03:16.400
Yeah. Let's unpack that really quickly.

03:16.600 --> 03:24.240
For anyone who hasn't been, you know, deep in the trenches of gaming or tech regulation, a loot box is essentially a digital slot machine.

03:24.440 --> 03:25.540
That's the best way to describe it.

03:25.540 --> 03:34.420
You pay real money to open a virtual crate and you have a randomized chance of getting something incredibly rare or much more likely just absolute garbage.

03:34.420 --> 03:37.060
Right. And we've spent years talking about this.

03:37.780 --> 03:40.980
We've heavily scrutinized the behavioral psychology behind it.

03:41.080 --> 03:42.060
It's everywhere in the news.

03:42.320 --> 03:48.980
Because the behavioral mechanism there is an intermittent variable ratio reinforcement schedule.

03:49.220 --> 03:51.540
Which sounds incredibly academic, but...

03:51.540 --> 03:55.740
Well, it just means the uncertainty of the reward is the primary driver of the behavior.

03:56.080 --> 03:58.780
You don't know when the payout is coming, so you keep pulling the lever.

03:58.880 --> 03:59.700
Right. Right.

03:59.700 --> 04:11.260
The researcher points out that this specific mechanism has been heavily and rightfully analyzed because it has been linked at the population level to problem gambling scores.

04:11.500 --> 04:15.720
The reward schedule literally hijacks the dopamine system.

04:15.720 --> 04:26.480
So the panic happened. Right. Regulators stepped in. But the legal response internationally has been just a complete mess. It's a total patchwork of contradictory rulings.

04:26.560 --> 04:37.160
Oh, it is incredibly fragmented. I mean, consider Belgium. The gaming commission there looked at certain paid loot boxes and classified them definitively as gambling under their national law.

04:37.360 --> 04:38.720
So they basically banned them.

04:38.920 --> 04:39.640
Effectively, yes.

04:39.700 --> 04:39.900
Yeah.

04:40.160 --> 04:42.300
Banned. But then you contrast that with the Netherlands.

04:42.540 --> 04:43.420
Right. Right. Next door.

04:43.420 --> 04:52.180
Exactly. Their gambling authority attempted a very similar crackdown. But in 2022, the country's highest administrative court actually overturned it on appeal.

04:52.480 --> 04:54.300
Wait, they overturned the ban?

04:54.620 --> 05:03.220
They did. The reasoning was fascinating, actually. They ruled that the loot box was not a standalone game of chance that could be legally divorced from the larger game itself.

05:03.320 --> 05:03.780
Oh, I see.

05:03.960 --> 05:11.280
Yeah. They said it was an integrated mechanic and therefore didn't fit the strict legal definition of an unlicensed casino.

05:11.280 --> 05:20.420
That is wild. Meanwhile, the UK conducted this massive government review looking at all the exact same evidence and they basically threw their hands up.

05:20.540 --> 05:22.420
Yeah. The UK approach was very different.

05:22.420 --> 05:29.460
They found a clear association between spending money on these digital boxes and real world gambling harm.

05:29.680 --> 05:33.920
But because they couldn't prove a direct, undeniable, causal link.

05:33.980 --> 05:38.660
Like, does the box cause the gambling or are people prone to gambling just drawn to the box?

05:38.660 --> 05:41.060
Exactly. Because they couldn't prove that, they just punted.

05:41.280 --> 05:44.060
They opted for industry-led protective measures.

05:44.220 --> 05:49.080
They basically asked the companies profiting from the boxes to, you know, regulate themselves.

05:49.080 --> 05:50.920
Which historically goes so well.

05:51.060 --> 06:02.980
Right. And just to illustrate how chaotic this landscape still is, that same year, you had a coalition of over 20 consumer protection groups spanning 18 different European countries demanding harmonized, unified regulation.

06:03.360 --> 06:05.920
They were calling the design inherently predatory.

06:05.920 --> 06:21.560
Yeah. But the critical takeaway from all of this international legal maneuvering is that the entire regulatory framework, the hearings, the bans, the appeals, is singularly obsessed with the probability of a financial transaction.

06:21.760 --> 06:24.560
It is all about the money. Always.

06:24.560 --> 06:30.360
Which brings us to the core thesis of Flyxion's essay and why this paper is so vital for you to understand.

06:30.900 --> 06:36.400
Focusing entirely on loot boxes is like investigating a casino because you suspect they have rigged the roulette tables.

06:36.560 --> 06:36.680
Right.

06:36.920 --> 06:44.760
But completely ignoring the fact that the casino management has chained the pit boss to the front desk so they can literally never go home to sleep.

06:44.760 --> 06:48.800
That analogy perfectly captures the blind spot in our current discourse.

06:49.560 --> 06:58.100
The paper argues that temporal extraction, the systematic extraction of a person's time and social energy, is completely orthogonal to monetary extraction.

06:58.380 --> 07:00.760
Orthogonal. Meaning they don't depend on each other at all.

07:00.900 --> 07:04.520
Exactly. They are mathematically and structurally independent of one another.

07:04.520 --> 07:19.060
So, meaning you could have a piece of software that is 100% free with absolutely no loot boxes, no microtransactions, no slot machine mechanics whatsoever, and it could still systematically ruin your sleep schedule and hijack your life.

07:19.140 --> 07:28.980
Precisely. Or you could have a turn-based game, something like Digital Chess, that is packed to the brim with aggressive monetization and costs a fortune to play.

07:29.420 --> 07:30.700
But you can still walk away from it.

07:30.700 --> 07:33.160
Right. It places zero temporal pressure on the player.

07:33.160 --> 07:38.160
You can walk away from that chess match for a week, and the board remains exactly as you left it.

07:38.300 --> 07:39.100
Nothing changes.

07:39.740 --> 07:46.240
Conversely, you can have a completely unmonetized system that structurally forbids you from ever truly resting.

07:46.980 --> 07:54.140
By lumping all of this under the vague, unhelpful umbrella term of addictive design, we completely miss the trap.

07:54.220 --> 07:58.640
We miss the trap, and we end up falsely putting the burden entirely on the user.

07:58.640 --> 08:06.180
We look at someone who is exhausted, who is checking their phone at 3 a.m., and we say, wow, they just lack personal self-control.

08:06.360 --> 08:07.840
Which is such an unfair assessment.

08:08.100 --> 08:08.540
It is.

08:08.860 --> 08:15.680
We don't look at the software architecture and say, wait a minute, this environment is mathematically structured to penalize rest.

08:15.680 --> 08:22.920
To understand exactly how a digital environment penalizes rest, we have to look at the specific evolution of the software.

08:23.540 --> 08:31.600
The researcher zeroes in on how we moved from a game being a bounded, optional session to a continuous, inescapable obligation.

08:32.080 --> 08:37.380
Right. How did digital environments go from something we occasionally visit to something we permanently inhabit?

08:37.720 --> 08:40.900
The paper focuses on a very specific genre to illustrate this.

08:41.120 --> 08:44.180
We aren't talking about playing a quick round of a racing game on your couch.

08:44.180 --> 08:45.420
No, definitely not.

08:45.520 --> 08:51.260
We are talking about real-time, globally synchronous, alliance-based multiplayer titles.

08:51.380 --> 08:58.360
These are like massive territory control strategy games or complex city builders, usually played on mobile phones, right?

08:58.420 --> 08:59.360
Yes, mostly mobile.

08:59.720 --> 09:04.980
Where events are happening continuously across a player base distributed over every single time zone on Earth.

09:05.060 --> 09:08.180
The contrast with older multiplayer models is really vital here.

09:08.180 --> 09:15.740
If we look back at the modem era of gaming, like the late 90s, playing a real-time strategy game was a discreet, optional session.

09:16.100 --> 09:20.300
You dial in, you connect with another player, you fight your digital match.

09:20.460 --> 09:26.380
And the match fundamentally ends when the connection is severed, you log off, and the world pauses.

09:26.380 --> 09:30.860
But in these modern, persistent games, the match doesn't exist anymore.

09:31.340 --> 09:33.740
The match has been entirely replaced by a membership.

09:34.080 --> 09:34.600
A membership?

09:35.080 --> 09:35.500
Yes.

09:35.900 --> 09:40.580
When you log into one of these synchronous alliance games, you aren't just connecting for a quick skirmish.

09:40.940 --> 09:44.160
You are joining a standing, permanent collective.

09:44.660 --> 09:47.220
You join a guild, an alliance, a clan.

09:47.220 --> 09:50.720
And that collective continues to exist in the game world.

09:51.120 --> 09:53.520
It continues to accrue social obligations.

09:53.980 --> 10:01.700
And most importantly, it continues to face hostile threats from other players, regardless of whether you are personally awake and logged in.

10:01.960 --> 10:04.460
The researcher actually formalizes this shift.

10:04.920 --> 10:09.320
They identify four specific pillars of what they term structural unfinishability.

10:10.000 --> 10:15.320
For a system to create this inescapable trap, all four of these conditions must be present simultaneously.

10:15.320 --> 10:16.420
Okay, let's go through these.

10:16.420 --> 10:17.200
What's the first pillar?

10:17.500 --> 10:21.580
The first is that consequential state changes must be able to occur during your absence.

10:22.340 --> 10:25.360
The world simply does not freeze when you close the application.

10:25.660 --> 10:27.760
Okay, so the clock is always ticking.

10:27.940 --> 10:28.880
Things are always moving.

10:29.280 --> 10:31.080
What is the second pillar?

10:31.220 --> 10:40.760
The second is that the timing of those consequential changes is substantially controlled by other actors or by the server itself, not by the user.

10:40.960 --> 10:43.080
You do not dictate when the event happens.

10:43.080 --> 10:44.720
You can't schedule the crisis.

10:45.020 --> 10:45.840
It happens to you.

10:46.180 --> 10:46.320
Right.

10:46.420 --> 10:51.580
The third condition is that the resulting losses from those changes must be difficult to reverse.

10:51.580 --> 10:57.780
If something goes wrong while you are away, it cannot simply be undone with the click of a button.

10:58.020 --> 10:58.940
No easy undo.

10:59.540 --> 10:59.980
Got it.

11:00.200 --> 11:00.680
And the fourth.

11:00.900 --> 11:11.080
The fourth, which is perhaps the most psychologically potent, is that the responsibility for preventing those losses is socially attributed specifically to you by the other members of your group.

11:11.080 --> 11:11.520
Wow.

11:11.520 --> 11:18.480
When you lay those four pillars out like that, it becomes terrifyingly clear that these software titles aren't really functioning as games anymore.

11:18.480 --> 11:19.080
Not at all.

11:19.080 --> 11:19.220
Not at all.

11:19.220 --> 11:19.480
Not at all.

11:19.480 --> 11:22.520
They are operating as fully-fledged social media platforms.

11:22.520 --> 11:25.180
The paper highlights this beautifully.

11:25.180 --> 11:37.340
When a piece of software combines persistent user identity, dedicated communication channels, a complex social graph, and standing group membership, you have built a social network.

11:37.340 --> 11:40.500
And we already know the psychological toll of social networks.

11:40.500 --> 11:45.460
I mean, they have long been criticized for manufacturing a sense of costly absence.

11:45.460 --> 11:46.420
Yeah, FOMO.

11:46.780 --> 11:47.220
Exactly.

11:47.700 --> 11:50.840
We are all intimately familiar with the fear of missing out.

11:51.540 --> 11:55.160
Missing a cultural moment in a group chat feels socially isolating.

11:55.720 --> 12:01.700
But this architecture takes that familiar social FOMO and weaponizes it with a severe mechanical penalty.

12:01.840 --> 12:04.480
It's not just that you missed an inside joke while you were disconnected.

12:04.780 --> 12:05.120
No.

12:05.480 --> 12:15.140
It's that while you were eating dinner with your family, your alliance's virtual territory, which took hundreds of hours of coordinated human labor to construct, was permanently destroyed.

12:15.140 --> 12:17.980
Specifically because you were not at your screen to activate the defenses?

12:18.560 --> 12:23.100
The social consequence and the mechanical consequence lock together perfectly.

12:23.400 --> 12:24.440
It's a double bind.

12:24.880 --> 12:26.100
You are letting down your friends.

12:26.120 --> 12:26.340
Yeah.

12:26.500 --> 12:29.060
And you are losing your digital assets simultaneously.

12:29.520 --> 12:32.740
Now, it's one thing to describe this trap emotionally, right?

12:32.880 --> 12:37.340
But what absolutely blew my mind about this paper is how the researcher quantified it.

12:37.480 --> 12:37.880
The math.

12:38.020 --> 12:38.300
Yes.

12:38.520 --> 12:43.840
They actually created a mathematical formula to measure this exact feeling of being trapped.

12:43.840 --> 12:47.120
They took human anxiety and turned it into an equation.

12:47.520 --> 12:49.300
They termed it the exposure integral.

12:50.080 --> 12:59.040
And while introducing math into social psychology can sometimes feel, I don't know, overly abstract, this specific formula is wonderfully intuitive.

12:59.320 --> 13:00.100
It really is.

13:00.260 --> 13:04.520
It calculates a participant's temporal exposure over a given interval of time.

13:04.940 --> 13:10.520
It measures with precision exactly how vulnerable you become the moment you put your device down.

13:10.520 --> 13:16.180
Let's break down the variables in this formula because understanding this is like seeing the matrix for the first time.

13:16.340 --> 13:16.720
Let's do it.

13:16.880 --> 13:21.160
If you've ever felt that pit in your stomach when closing an app, this formula explains exactly why.

13:21.400 --> 13:22.720
There are four key variables.

13:22.920 --> 13:24.080
The first one is lambda.

13:24.540 --> 13:27.820
Lambda represents the hazard rate or the event intensity.

13:28.440 --> 13:33.600
It is the expected rate of consequential events that happens specifically when you are absent.

13:34.000 --> 13:36.460
Crucially, this is not a measure of server uptime.

13:36.460 --> 13:41.260
It doesn't matter if the server is running 24-7 if nothing is actually happening.

13:41.800 --> 13:47.340
Lambda measures whether attacks, crises, or critical events are actively being launched while you are away.

13:47.840 --> 13:49.600
So lambda is the active threat level.

13:49.860 --> 13:52.520
The second variable is L, which stands for loss.

13:52.680 --> 13:52.840
Right.

13:53.180 --> 13:56.780
If a hazard event occurs while you are away, how devastating is the outcome?

13:57.140 --> 14:05.460
If you are merely losing a few minor resources that you can easily replace in five minutes of play tomorrow, the L variable is very low.

14:05.460 --> 14:07.480
It's an annoyance, not a catastrophe.

14:07.780 --> 14:08.160
Exactly.

14:08.860 --> 14:18.560
However, if an attack while you sleep results in the destruction of your alliance's capital city, wiping out six months of coordinated effort, the potential loss is massive.

14:18.880 --> 14:22.100
The higher the potential destruction, the higher the L variable.

14:22.320 --> 14:23.620
Then we get to the human element.

14:23.940 --> 14:25.940
The third variable is our responsibility.

14:26.560 --> 14:29.860
And this one hits hard because I think we've all experienced this outside of gaming.

14:30.320 --> 14:30.660
Oh, definitely.

14:30.660 --> 14:35.060
Imagine being assigned a massive presentation for a college group project.

14:35.380 --> 14:40.100
If you are just the person assigned to read a few slides, your responsibility is pretty low.

14:40.560 --> 14:43.320
If you sleep in, the group can probably cover for you.

14:43.500 --> 14:43.720
Right.

14:43.820 --> 14:44.880
Your absence isn't fatal.

14:45.520 --> 14:52.900
But if you are the one holding the USB drive with the only copy of the presentation on it, your R variable is astronomical.

14:52.900 --> 15:00.600
If you aren't there, the entire group fails and the blame falls squarely, 100% on your shoulders.

15:01.180 --> 15:03.080
That is a perfect translation of the concept.

15:03.720 --> 15:10.700
In these digital environments, R measures how much the Standing Collective blames you specifically for a failure.

15:10.840 --> 15:12.360
So if you're just a random recruit.

15:12.360 --> 15:16.940
If you are a low-ranking recruit in a massive alliance, your R is negligible.

15:17.280 --> 15:18.640
No one expects you to hold the line.

15:19.080 --> 15:24.340
But if you have been promoted to lead diplomat or defensive coordinator, your R is incredibly high.

15:25.000 --> 15:28.160
The system and the social group expects you to be present.

15:28.640 --> 15:30.960
And the final variable locks the whole thing in place.

15:31.440 --> 15:32.460
Use substitutability.

15:33.040 --> 15:35.500
Basically, how hard is it to delegate your job?

15:35.700 --> 15:38.620
How difficult is it to find someone else to cover your shift?

15:38.620 --> 15:46.160
In many of these complex systems, substitutability is artificially constrained by the software itself.

15:46.280 --> 15:49.220
Meaning, the game literally won't let someone else do it.

15:49.300 --> 15:49.520
Right.

15:49.860 --> 15:58.320
You might hold a specific rank in the game's code that grants you unique permissions buttons you can press that literally no one else in the alliance has access to.

15:58.900 --> 16:04.940
If you are the only one who can activate the defensive shield, your substitutability is effectively zero.

16:04.940 --> 16:10.900
Which means your U variable, the difficulty of replacement, is incredibly high.

16:11.080 --> 16:21.580
So the exposure integral formula takes the active threat, the potential devastation of the loss, the social blame placed on you, and the impossibility of handing the task to someone else, and it multiplies them.

16:21.780 --> 16:22.860
Yep. All four.

16:23.020 --> 16:26.740
If all four of those variables are high, your exposure is massive.

16:27.060 --> 16:30.380
The math literally dictates that you cannot safely step away.

16:30.540 --> 16:32.940
It creates a quantifiable metric for unfinishability.

16:32.940 --> 16:40.440
But let me test the boundaries of this formula for a second, because I can think of games that run 204.7 that don't feel like this.

16:40.500 --> 16:41.380
Okay. Let's hear it.

16:41.760 --> 16:45.160
What if I rent a persistent Minecraft server for my friends?

16:45.660 --> 16:54.760
I go to work, and while I'm at the office, the server is running, time is passing, and my friends are online building a massive, beautiful castle.

16:55.200 --> 16:55.840
Sounds fun.

16:55.840 --> 17:01.320
It is. The world is persistent, it's real time, and changes are happening without me.

17:01.760 --> 17:04.520
Am I being structurally extracted by that game?

17:04.900 --> 17:10.540
The formula beautifully accounts for that exact scenario, and the answer is no, you are not being extracted.

17:10.700 --> 17:11.680
Because of the variables.

17:11.940 --> 17:12.220
Exactly.

17:12.740 --> 17:19.200
In that cooperative creative environment, the L variable, the loss, is effectively zero.

17:19.200 --> 17:25.000
Nothing of value is being destroyed while you are at work, your friends are simply adding to the world.

17:25.100 --> 17:25.960
Ah, right.

17:26.160 --> 17:34.100
Furthermore, your variable responsibility is virtually non-existent, because no one in the group is blaming you for failing to defend against an attack that isn't happening.

17:34.240 --> 17:37.140
So the world persists, but the exposure drops to zero.

17:37.140 --> 17:46.840
The formula proves that structural unfinishability is not an inherent property of a server simply running around the clock, it is a relational, social, and structural property.

17:46.920 --> 17:48.080
That makes so much sense.

17:48.340 --> 17:52.820
It depends entirely on what is mechanically at stake and who is socially held accountable.

17:53.720 --> 18:01.200
If the equation drops to a tolerable level, you have achieved what the researcher calls a safe disengagement interval.

18:01.380 --> 18:03.460
Meaning I can close the app without panicking.

18:03.460 --> 18:11.400
Right. You can unilaterally decide to close the software, go about your life, go to sleep, and suffer no intolerable consequences.

18:11.680 --> 18:16.160
Which brings us to the most biological, visceral part of this entire analysis.

18:16.320 --> 18:17.340
We just mentioned sleep.

18:17.980 --> 18:23.860
When you are playing on a global server, what happens to that lambda variable, the active threat rate?

18:23.960 --> 18:25.520
This is where it gets really insidious.

18:25.520 --> 18:33.340
The paper introduces a concept called circadian vulnerability, and it explains how these systems literally weaponize human biology.

18:33.460 --> 18:39.080
To fully grasp the nature of this, we have to look at the baseline biological constraints of being a human being.

18:39.800 --> 18:46.540
We require sleep roughly every 24 hours, and our sleep cycles are inherently tied to our local geographic time zones.

18:46.680 --> 18:48.300
We only have periodic availability.

18:48.500 --> 18:53.000
Right. When the sun sets where you live, eventually your body demands that you disconnect.

18:53.460 --> 18:57.840
I cannot keep my eyes open indefinitely. Biology always wins.

18:57.840 --> 19:04.220
But the digital architecture does not sleep, and more importantly, the adversary population does not sleep.

19:04.680 --> 19:08.760
The paper outlines the mechanics of adversarial availability.

19:09.140 --> 19:10.100
Okay. Explain that.

19:10.340 --> 19:16.260
Imagine you are playing a competitive game where every single one of your opponents lives in your exact same time zone.

19:16.260 --> 19:22.800
In that scenario, the active threat level, the hazard rate rises and falls with the sun.

19:23.140 --> 19:25.840
Because everyone is on the same biological clock.

19:25.980 --> 19:30.220
Exactly. You all go to work at the same time. You all go to sleep roughly at the same time.

19:30.840 --> 19:36.360
During the night, the threat drops to near zero because everyone is biologically compelled to rest.

19:36.600 --> 19:41.480
But these Alliance games aren't regional. They are explicitly designed to be globally synchronous.

19:41.480 --> 19:45.580
And that global dispersion fundamentally alters the math of the threat.

19:46.080 --> 19:51.900
As the adversary population becomes distributed across all time zones, players in London, Tokyo, New York, Sydney,

19:52.560 --> 19:56.380
their individual peaks and troughs of biological availability cancel each other out.

19:56.460 --> 19:58.420
The researcher calls this the flattening effect.

19:58.580 --> 19:59.600
The flattening effect, yes.

19:59.720 --> 20:01.960
So let's visualize this for you, the listener.

20:02.620 --> 20:05.320
Paint a picture of what this actually looks like for a player.

20:05.920 --> 20:07.380
Imagine charting this on a graph.

20:07.380 --> 20:12.520
A single player's availability is a massive wave peaking during their local evening

20:12.520 --> 20:15.860
and dropping into a deep valley during their local night.

20:16.060 --> 20:17.600
Standard bell curve kind of thing.

20:17.840 --> 20:18.060
Right.

20:18.580 --> 20:23.340
But if you take thousands of players from every time zone and stack their waves on top of each other,

20:23.820 --> 20:28.560
the aggregate availability of the enemy flattens out into a constant horizontal line.

20:28.680 --> 20:30.260
Because the peaks fill in the valleys.

20:30.480 --> 20:31.040
Exactly.

20:31.600 --> 20:35.360
The variance in threat drops as geographic dispersion increases.

20:35.360 --> 20:44.620
The sun is always rising somewhere, which means someone, somewhere, is always at peak availability, ready to attack.

20:44.720 --> 20:52.300
Meaning my 3.0 a.m., when my body is literally forcing me into REM sleep and my cognitive function is at its absolute lowest,

20:52.460 --> 20:55.540
is an attacker in a different hemisphere is 3.0 p.m.

20:55.600 --> 20:55.780
Yep.

20:56.020 --> 20:58.040
They just finished a cup of coffee, they are wide awake,

20:58.220 --> 21:02.180
and they are launching a massive coordinated assault on your digital territory.

21:02.180 --> 21:04.920
What is truly fascinating, and perhaps a bit chilling,

21:05.360 --> 21:10.080
is that the attackers aren't necessarily being malicious about your specific sleep schedule.

21:10.260 --> 21:10.760
Not at all.

21:11.420 --> 21:13.060
The player in Tokyo isn't thinking,

21:13.360 --> 21:16.500
I'm going to ruin this person's physical health by attacking them at 4 a.m.

21:16.660 --> 21:19.720
They are just playing the game during their normal afternoon.

21:20.020 --> 21:22.180
It is simply the cold math of global dispersion.

21:23.060 --> 21:24.900
The researcher articulates this perfectly.

21:25.040 --> 21:25.400
They say,

21:25.400 --> 21:31.240
Circadian absence is converted into a strategically exploitable regularity.

21:31.380 --> 21:31.780
Man.

21:32.260 --> 21:38.020
Your biological imperative to sleep becomes a massive, predictable, tactical vulnerability,

21:38.780 --> 21:41.480
purely as a function of the server's global geography.

21:42.000 --> 21:44.980
No amount of clever user interface design,

21:45.120 --> 21:47.540
and no amount of adjusting the brightness on your screen,

21:47.920 --> 21:49.300
can patch human biology.

21:49.300 --> 21:53.540
The structure of the game has taken a non-negotiable biological necessity

21:53.540 --> 21:56.340
and transformed it into a strategic weakness.

21:56.700 --> 21:59.680
You are being mathematically punished for needing to be unconscious.

22:00.160 --> 22:02.360
Which brings us to the inevitable breaking point.

22:03.240 --> 22:06.660
If the threat is constant, and if sleep is biologically mandatory,

22:07.460 --> 22:09.480
why doesn't a player just look at their phone,

22:09.580 --> 22:12.500
say, you know what, this is just a game, I don't care, and close the app?

22:12.560 --> 22:13.900
That's the logical question, right?

22:13.900 --> 22:20.720
Why do functional adults set physical alarm clocks for 4.00 a.m. to wake up and defend a digital territory?

22:21.200 --> 22:23.680
Because of the R and the U variables we talked about.

22:24.780 --> 22:26.060
Responsibility and delegability.

22:26.060 --> 22:28.620
We are talking about the burden of leadership,

22:29.340 --> 22:32.900
and a concept that early internet scholars called Playber.

22:33.080 --> 22:33.540
Playber.

22:33.640 --> 22:35.920
Yes, it's a portmanteau of play and labor.

22:36.300 --> 22:39.000
It's drawn from foundational game studies literature,

22:39.540 --> 22:42.820
pointing to researchers like Kuklitch, Levine, Nardi, and Yee.

22:42.820 --> 22:43.960
Oh, I've read some of that.

22:44.080 --> 22:46.740
It describes a phenomenon in complex online environments,

22:47.240 --> 22:50.920
where leading a large collective of players routinely crosses the line from being

22:50.920 --> 22:56.640
recreational fun into being unpaid, intensely demanding managerial work.

22:56.880 --> 23:00.900
If anyone remembers the early days of World of Warcraft, you know exactly what this looks like.

23:01.460 --> 23:05.720
Guild leaders in those early MMOs were essentially running unpaid small businesses.

23:05.900 --> 23:06.980
They absolutely were.

23:07.120 --> 23:10.600
They were handling human resources, managing complex schedules,

23:10.600 --> 23:14.360
resolving interpersonal disputes, all completely for free.

23:14.680 --> 23:20.060
But Flyxion makes a vital structural distinction between a game like World of Warcraft

23:20.060 --> 23:24.920
and the modern, globally synchronous territory games we're analyzing today.

23:25.060 --> 23:25.860
How so?

23:26.200 --> 23:30.260
In older MMOs, the highest consequence content, say,

23:30.260 --> 23:35.700
a massive 40-person dungeon raid was scheduled by the participating group.

23:36.560 --> 23:39.480
The guild leader carried a massive organizational burden, yes,

23:39.860 --> 23:41.880
but they possessed the agency to say,

23:42.300 --> 23:44.700
we are conducting this raid on Tuesday at 8 p.m.

23:44.900 --> 23:45.680
They picked the night.

23:45.880 --> 23:47.580
They controlled the temporal boundary of the event.

23:47.660 --> 23:48.400
They opted in.

23:48.680 --> 23:51.600
But in these globally synchronous alliance games,

23:51.820 --> 23:53.460
you don't control the schedule at all.

23:53.640 --> 23:54.420
Not even a little bit.

23:54.600 --> 23:56.660
The opponent initiates the high-stakes event,

23:56.660 --> 23:59.540
and because of the global flattening effect we just talked about,

23:59.660 --> 24:02.940
they often initiate it precisely when you are most biologically vulnerable.

24:03.200 --> 24:04.580
You are reacting, not scheduling.

24:04.980 --> 24:08.280
The schedule is dictated entirely by adversarial action,

24:08.860 --> 24:10.020
not by mutual consent.

24:10.400 --> 24:12.960
And when that 4.0 a.m. attack alarm goes off,

24:13.380 --> 24:17.600
the burden of responding does not fall equally on every member of the alliance.

24:17.720 --> 24:17.920
Right.

24:18.020 --> 24:19.060
The recruits can sleep.

24:19.340 --> 24:19.740
Exactly.

24:19.740 --> 24:24.640
It falls disproportionately and crushingly on specific roles,

24:24.980 --> 24:28.320
the alliance leaders, the diplomats, the defense coordinators.

24:28.560 --> 24:30.880
And it's crucial to realize that this isn't an accident.

24:31.640 --> 24:36.080
The game's platform infrastructure intentionally manufactures that responsibility.

24:36.260 --> 24:37.720
It is baked right into the interface.

24:37.840 --> 24:40.820
A software provides the complex hierarchical rank systems,

24:41.300 --> 24:44.860
the dedicated officer chat channels, the public reputation metrics.

24:44.860 --> 24:49.860
The game literally bestows upon you a shiny digital badge that says Defense General.

24:50.160 --> 24:52.860
And the moment you accept it, the social guilt is locked into place.

24:52.920 --> 24:53.020
Yeah.

24:53.320 --> 24:55.640
If you sleep through that 4.0 a.m. attack,

24:55.800 --> 24:57.740
you didn't just lose a few digital buildings.

24:57.940 --> 25:02.080
You let down 60 real human beings who are relying on you to push the button.

25:02.320 --> 25:06.120
If we route this back through our exposure integral formula,

25:06.760 --> 25:09.760
the system engineers an incredibly high raw responsibility

25:09.760 --> 25:12.020
and couples it with a very high U,

25:12.020 --> 25:15.680
because only certain ranked officers have the hard-coded permissions

25:15.680 --> 25:18.640
to activate defenses or declare counterattacks.

25:19.140 --> 25:21.260
So the substitutability is basically zero.

25:21.480 --> 25:21.640
Right.

25:22.580 --> 25:25.640
The pressure forcing a player to wake up in the middle of the night

25:25.640 --> 25:27.780
doesn't come from a fear of the server clock.

25:28.420 --> 25:31.540
It is driven by the deeply human evolutionary fear

25:31.540 --> 25:35.240
of letting down a real standing social group.

25:35.400 --> 25:36.860
It's social survival instinct.

25:37.140 --> 25:37.720
Exactly.

25:37.980 --> 25:39.780
The architecture takes the very worst,

25:39.780 --> 25:42.880
most anxiety-inducing aspects of social media FOMO,

25:43.300 --> 25:46.100
and it compounds them with material digital destruction.

25:46.440 --> 25:49.000
It is weaponized peer pressure, automated by code.

25:49.080 --> 25:49.980
That's a great way to put it.

25:50.040 --> 25:52.860
Which brings us to arguably the most provocative assertion

25:52.860 --> 25:53.880
in this entire essay.

25:54.300 --> 25:56.940
We've established that this structure creates a massive,

25:57.240 --> 25:59.460
sleep-depriving, anxiety-inducing burden,

25:59.980 --> 26:02.700
especially for the people stepping up to lead these alliances.

26:02.940 --> 26:03.180
Yes.

26:03.180 --> 26:06.120
But is it just a byproduct of badly designed mechanics?

26:06.120 --> 26:08.060
Or is it something more insidious?

26:08.240 --> 26:10.520
Is this actual economic extraction?

26:11.140 --> 26:13.240
This is a crucial intellectual distinction to make.

26:14.520 --> 26:17.800
The paper clearly states that structural unfinishability

26:17.800 --> 26:21.580
does not automatically equate to economic extraction.

26:21.640 --> 26:24.360
We have to separate the architecture from the economics.

26:24.640 --> 26:24.840
We do.

26:25.060 --> 26:27.660
We have to separate the architectural condition of the software

26:27.660 --> 26:29.920
from the economic outcome it produces.

26:29.920 --> 26:33.240
Right, because if I decide to program an open-source,

26:33.360 --> 26:35.300
non-profit territory game in my basement,

26:35.720 --> 26:38.380
and it happens to have these exact same stressful,

26:38.700 --> 26:41.480
unfinishable, 247 mechanics,

26:41.760 --> 26:43.480
I'm not extracting anything from anyone.

26:43.580 --> 26:46.180
No, you're just a guy who designed a really stressful hobby.

26:46.400 --> 26:46.660
Right.

26:47.180 --> 26:50.440
There is no corporate publisher profiting off the surplus value

26:50.440 --> 26:52.060
of my player's sleep deprivation.

26:52.160 --> 26:53.860
Extraction requires a beneficiary.

26:54.300 --> 26:56.020
It requires a one-way flow of value.

26:56.020 --> 26:59.240
So the researcher proposes a compelling economic hypothesis

26:59.240 --> 27:03.000
for how commercial games operating in this space generate revenue.

27:03.460 --> 27:05.020
Walk us through that causal chain.

27:05.260 --> 27:06.100
It operates like this.

27:06.360 --> 27:07.580
Player coordination, labor,

27:08.180 --> 27:11.020
all those alliance leaders waking up before 4 a.m.,

27:11.020 --> 27:13.880
managing stride sheets, resolving disputes, training recruits,

27:14.380 --> 27:16.900
that labor generates persistent social content.

27:17.220 --> 27:19.520
The players are making the game fun for each other.

27:19.660 --> 27:20.080
Exactly.

27:20.600 --> 27:22.020
They are the ones creating the dramatic rivalries,

27:22.020 --> 27:26.180
the community cohesion, the vibrant ecosystem of the game.

27:26.400 --> 27:27.840
The code is just an empty sandbox.

27:28.160 --> 27:29.760
The players are building the castles.

27:30.080 --> 27:33.820
And this unpaid social organization is the primary driver of retention.

27:34.800 --> 27:37.300
A cohesive, well-organized player base

27:37.300 --> 27:41.880
embedded in a web of social obligations does not quit the game.

27:41.980 --> 27:42.780
They can't quit.

27:42.940 --> 27:44.160
The guilt is too high.

27:44.260 --> 27:44.480
Right.

27:44.900 --> 27:46.860
And what does high retention drive

27:46.860 --> 27:50.260
for a commercial publisher operating a free-to-play model?

27:50.700 --> 27:51.740
Monetizable engagement.

27:52.060 --> 27:52.360
Precisely.

27:52.480 --> 27:53.980
The longer people stay logged in,

27:54.120 --> 27:55.780
the deeper their social ties become,

27:55.860 --> 27:59.640
the more likely they are to eventually spend real money on microtransactions.

27:59.860 --> 28:02.160
Or those loot boxes we discussed at the very beginning.

28:02.300 --> 28:02.820
It all connects.

28:03.120 --> 28:06.220
So the publisher is directly financially benefiting

28:06.220 --> 28:11.020
from the uncompensated, highly stressful labor of these alliance leaders.

28:11.640 --> 28:13.700
Let me try an analogy here, see if I have this right.

28:13.920 --> 28:14.380
Go for it.

28:14.380 --> 28:18.480
It's like a major corporation built a massive, state-of-the-art,

28:18.600 --> 28:20.600
but completely empty football stadium.

28:20.600 --> 28:23.020
They hand you the keys, and they say,

28:23.520 --> 28:24.880
hey, this is a fun game.

28:24.980 --> 28:25.740
Go nuts.

28:25.940 --> 28:26.640
Okay, I'm with you.

28:26.800 --> 28:32.460
But then they sit back and let you organically build the entire football franchise from scratch.

28:32.600 --> 28:33.760
You scout the players.

28:34.000 --> 28:35.420
You coach the daily practices.

28:35.780 --> 28:38.040
You manage the interpersonal locker room drama.

28:38.040 --> 28:40.660
You print and sell the tickets to the fans.

28:40.920 --> 28:41.400
All for free.

28:41.520 --> 28:41.680
Right.

28:41.740 --> 28:45.300
You do it all entirely for free because you're passionate about the game.

28:45.900 --> 28:48.640
But the corporation, they own the stadium.

28:48.640 --> 28:52.820
So they keep 100% of the profits from the concession stands,

28:53.140 --> 28:55.020
the parking, and the television rights.

28:55.020 --> 28:57.420
That is an exceptionally apt analogy.

28:57.860 --> 29:02.400
The players are providing the complex structural organization that makes the stadium worth visiting

29:02.400 --> 29:07.920
in the first place, but the publisher is capturing the economic surplus generated by that organization.

29:08.060 --> 29:10.080
It's brilliant and kind of evil.

29:10.080 --> 29:16.640
The players' coordination labor is an uncompensated yet utterly vital input to the game's commercial operation.

29:17.360 --> 29:20.320
That dynamic is the core of the extraction argument.

29:20.660 --> 29:24.020
We have spent a lot of time discussing this in the abstract, right?

29:24.020 --> 29:27.780
We've gone through the formulas, the biological realities, the economic theory.

29:27.920 --> 29:28.420
We have.

29:28.420 --> 29:32.260
But what does this actually look like when you download an app right now?

29:33.260 --> 29:36.880
The researcher grounds all of this theory in a specific case study,

29:37.460 --> 29:41.280
a mobile game called Last Shelter, Survival.

29:41.520 --> 29:44.680
The paper introduces this case study with commendable precision.

29:45.600 --> 29:47.960
The researcher is clear that examining Last Shelter,

29:48.620 --> 29:52.300
Survival is not an attempt to prove that every single synchronous game

29:52.300 --> 29:57.460
produces identical, measurable, harmful effects on a global population level.

29:57.460 --> 29:59.440
So it's not a blanket condemnation.

29:59.660 --> 30:04.040
Right. Rather, it serves to ground the abstract architecture we've been dissecting

30:04.040 --> 30:07.680
into a concrete, observable piece of commercial software.

30:08.100 --> 30:12.200
For someone who has never seen an ad for it, what exactly is Last Shelter?

30:12.800 --> 30:13.280
Survival.

30:13.580 --> 30:16.780
According to the developer's own storefront listings and promotional material,

30:17.200 --> 30:18.660
it's a real-time strategy title.

30:19.240 --> 30:21.820
The gameplay loop is built around persistent base development,

30:22.400 --> 30:26.780
complex alliance formation, and continuous worldwide conflict between players.

30:26.780 --> 30:27.520
Sound familiar?

30:27.980 --> 30:28.240
Very.

30:28.740 --> 30:32.000
It contains persistent messaging, global chat functionality,

30:32.240 --> 30:36.480
and yes, it is heavily monetized through in-app purchases and loot boxes.

30:36.680 --> 30:37.420
And the age rating?

30:37.980 --> 30:41.840
Notably, and perhaps most concerningly given our discussion of sleep deprivation,

30:42.160 --> 30:44.460
it carries an age rating of 13 and older.

30:44.760 --> 30:48.640
A 13-year-old can legally download this and be subjected to this architecture.

30:48.860 --> 30:50.140
Keep that in mind, everyone listening.

30:50.140 --> 30:56.860
So how does the structural unfinishability actually manifest in the mechanics of this specific game?

30:57.500 --> 31:00.700
The paper directs our attention to the game's competitive structure,

31:01.300 --> 31:06.140
specifically its reliance on recurring, multi-day competitive events.

31:06.600 --> 31:10.820
A prime example highlighted in the research is their state-versus-state server competition.

31:10.920 --> 31:11.720
State-versus-state.

31:11.720 --> 31:16.220
Yes. According to extensive community-maintained guides and player wikis,

31:16.660 --> 31:19.840
these events run on continuous, roughly 48-hour cycles.

31:20.040 --> 31:21.920
48 hours. I want to pause on that.

31:21.980 --> 31:22.520
It's a long time.

31:22.740 --> 31:28.480
Human beings literally cannot stay away for 48 hours without suffering severe, measurable cognitive impairment.

31:28.940 --> 31:33.560
The military uses 48-hour sleep deprivation as a stressor in training.

31:33.940 --> 31:36.780
So how does the game justify this?

31:36.780 --> 31:43.760
Yes. Surely a piece of entertainment software has some built-in mechanism for players to take a break without losing everything.

31:43.760 --> 31:47.480
They do offer a mitigation tool, which is common in the genre.

31:47.960 --> 31:49.260
The shield mechanic.

31:49.320 --> 31:50.240
The shield. Okay.

31:50.380 --> 31:55.100
A player can spend resources to activate a temporary localized defensive shield

31:55.100 --> 32:00.240
that protects their individual base from being attacked while they sleep, go to school, or go to work.

32:00.520 --> 32:03.460
Okay. Well, at first glance, that sounds like it solves the problem.

32:03.460 --> 32:07.780
You pop a shield, you go to sleep, the exposure formula drops to zero.

32:08.320 --> 32:12.260
Not quite. And this is where the nuance afflictions analysis really shines.

32:12.980 --> 32:17.020
The paper critically analyzes the functional limits of this shield mechanic.

32:17.320 --> 32:17.780
How so?

32:18.120 --> 32:21.600
Well, yes, it effectively protects the individual player's personal assets,

32:21.740 --> 32:24.580
it lowers their personal L or loss variable,

32:24.940 --> 32:29.580
but these shields are often subject to cool-down periods or escalating costs,

32:30.020 --> 32:32.020
meaning you cannot rely on them constantly.

32:32.020 --> 32:33.680
You can't just be shielded forever.

32:34.180 --> 32:37.500
Right. But more importantly, while it protects an isolated base,

32:37.680 --> 32:43.580
it does absolutely nothing to relieve the collective coordination responsibility placed on the Alliance leadership.

32:43.840 --> 32:46.880
Ah. Because the event itself is still running.

32:47.060 --> 32:51.560
The 48-hour global war is still happening, whether your personal house is shielded or not.

32:51.560 --> 32:56.280
Exactly. If you are the Alliance leader or the primary defense coordinator,

32:56.680 --> 33:04.020
putting a shield on your personal base doesn't stop the enemy Alliance from systematically dismantling the rest of your Alliance's unshielded territory.

33:04.260 --> 33:06.400
Your team is still getting slaughtered.

33:06.500 --> 33:12.360
Yes. The R variable, your responsibility for the well-being of the group, remains incredibly high,

33:12.800 --> 33:14.980
demanding your attention for the full 48 hours.

33:15.500 --> 33:19.640
The shield is an individual solution to a structural collective problem.

33:19.640 --> 33:23.180
I know you always look for the limits of the data, so let's be rigorous here.

33:23.500 --> 33:26.680
What are the evidentiary limits of this specific case study?

33:27.280 --> 33:32.060
What does looking at last shelter survival actually prove, and what does it not prove?

33:32.340 --> 33:33.540
That's an important question.

33:33.980 --> 33:37.160
Flyxion is explicitly careful about the evidentiary boundaries here.

33:38.460 --> 33:44.540
Analyzing a storefront listing, a feature set, and a community guide proves that the architecture exists.

33:44.540 --> 33:45.760
It proves the code is there.

33:46.120 --> 33:54.320
It proves that the technical preconditions for unfinishability, the persistent world state, the adversarially timed 48-hour competitive events,

33:54.680 --> 33:59.040
the hierarchical responsibility structure, are definitively coded into the software.

33:59.280 --> 33:59.540
Yeah.

33:59.820 --> 34:03.840
But it does not scientifically prove the epidemiological harm.

34:04.100 --> 34:08.720
Meaning we don't have medical records showing a million gamers actually losing sleep.

34:08.720 --> 34:09.400
Exactly.

34:09.760 --> 34:15.720
It does not prove that this specific game is causing chronic sleep disruption at a massive population level.

34:16.080 --> 34:21.780
To definitively say this software causes X,000 people to lose Y hours of sleep per week,

34:22.160 --> 34:25.740
you would need to conduct massive epidemiological player surveys.

34:25.960 --> 34:27.460
Clinical interviews, things like that.

34:27.580 --> 34:32.160
Clinical interviews and gain access to the developer's proprietary server activity logs

34:32.160 --> 34:35.080
to track login times against local time zones.

34:35.840 --> 34:37.020
The paper does not do that.

34:37.020 --> 34:39.140
Because they can't. The developers won't give that up.

34:39.340 --> 34:39.960
Of course not.

34:40.440 --> 34:46.100
What it does establish, undeniably, is that the preconditions for this extraction are built into the product.

34:46.580 --> 34:50.040
The trap is laid, the door is open, and the incentives are aligned.

34:50.460 --> 34:55.460
Whether and how often players are caught in it requires that next level of clinical study.

34:55.620 --> 35:03.360
So we have a digital architecture that functions like a psychological bear track disguised as a fun 13-plus mobile game.

35:03.360 --> 35:05.540
The technical structure is there.

35:06.200 --> 35:08.820
The financial incentives for the publisher are there.

35:09.100 --> 35:11.020
So how do we actually fix this?

35:11.140 --> 35:12.480
That is the million-dollar question.

35:12.680 --> 35:16.900
If we can't just slap a loot box odds disclosure sticker on the app and call it a day,

35:17.060 --> 35:20.780
how do we design our way out of structural unfinishability?

35:21.560 --> 35:24.040
This brings us to the remedy proposed in the paper.

35:24.500 --> 35:26.380
The concept of the right to absence.

35:26.380 --> 35:30.160
If the core problem is that players cannot safely disengage from the system,

35:30.740 --> 35:35.800
the design solution must be to structurally mathematically guarantee that disengagement is possible.

35:35.940 --> 35:36.460
Makes sense.

35:36.680 --> 35:39.340
The paper introduces a design criterion called the right to absence.

35:40.020 --> 35:43.680
It mandates that a system must allow a participant to invoke a bounded absence interval

35:43.680 --> 35:46.760
where their exposure drops below a tolerable threshold.

35:47.120 --> 35:51.380
And the absolute most critical word in that entire definition is unilaterally.

35:52.380 --> 35:53.020
Unilaterally, yeah.

35:53.040 --> 35:54.520
Meaning I don't have to form a committee,

35:55.020 --> 35:57.980
I don't have to beg my alliance leader for a night off,

35:58.020 --> 36:03.920
and I don't have to just cross my fingers and pray that the enemy team in Europe decides not to attack while I'm sleeping.

36:04.120 --> 36:06.900
If you have to rely on someone else's goodwill or inaction,

36:07.020 --> 36:09.400
you do not have a right to absence.

36:09.780 --> 36:11.820
I must be able to push a button that says,

36:11.940 --> 36:12.920
I am leaving,

36:13.320 --> 36:17.160
and the game's code must mathematically guarantee that the consequences stop.

36:17.480 --> 36:20.820
The diagnostic question that the paper poses is brilliantly simple,

36:20.820 --> 36:23.060
and the question we should apply to all software,

36:23.560 --> 36:25.860
can you press leave and have the consequences stop?

36:26.120 --> 36:29.280
If I'm playing a game of StarCraft on my PC from 1998,

36:29.580 --> 36:33.160
yes, I hit quit, the match dissolves, the consequences stop.

36:33.240 --> 36:33.480
Right.

36:33.580 --> 36:36.320
If I'm playing one of these modern persistent alliance games,

36:36.420 --> 36:38.040
no, the world keeps burning.

36:38.560 --> 36:42.440
So how do developers actually implement this right to absence

36:42.440 --> 36:46.640
without ruining the core concept of a persistent living world?

36:46.640 --> 36:49.900
It is an incredibly difficult design challenge,

36:50.200 --> 36:55.400
which is why the researcher meticulously walks through several flawed potential remedies

36:55.400 --> 36:59.680
to demonstrate how hard it is to balance persistence with safety.

36:59.880 --> 37:02.920
We've already discussed the failure of individual shields.

37:03.420 --> 37:04.840
They protect the solo player,

37:05.040 --> 37:08.740
but they leave the collective leaders completely burdened with responsibility.

37:08.740 --> 37:10.960
Okay, so what if we scale it up?

37:11.820 --> 37:13.720
What if the whole alliance gets a shield?

37:14.600 --> 37:17.440
You implement an alliance vulnerability window.

37:17.540 --> 37:19.680
Oh, like setting business hours for war.

37:19.760 --> 37:20.220
Exactly.

37:20.780 --> 37:21.700
The guild leader says,

37:22.060 --> 37:25.680
our alliance territory can only be attacked between 6 p.m. and 10 p.m.

37:26.080 --> 37:28.200
That sounds like a perfectly reasonable solution

37:28.200 --> 37:30.600
until you remember the geography of the player base.

37:30.660 --> 37:30.840
Right.

37:30.980 --> 37:32.160
Time zones ruin everything.

37:32.420 --> 37:34.620
These alliances are globally distributed.

37:34.620 --> 37:40.320
If an alliance leader sets the vulnerability window for 6 p.m. to 10 p.m. Eastern Standard Time,

37:40.500 --> 37:44.040
what happens to their alliance members who live in London or Tokyo?

37:44.180 --> 37:45.000
They are fast asleep.

37:45.220 --> 37:50.080
You've just shifted the temporal burden onto a minority time zone within your own group.

37:50.460 --> 37:54.020
You are essentially throwing your international teammates under the bus

37:54.020 --> 37:55.840
to protect your local sleep schedule.

37:56.240 --> 37:59.080
It fractures the global nature of the collective.

37:59.560 --> 37:59.900
Wow.

38:00.040 --> 38:01.960
Okay, the math of time zones ruins that one.

38:01.960 --> 38:05.240
What about asynchronous attack declarations?

38:05.400 --> 38:06.480
Like giving advance notice.

38:06.620 --> 38:06.800
Yeah.

38:07.180 --> 38:11.040
Like, if I want to attack your base, I can't just ambush you at 4 a.m.

38:11.120 --> 38:15.440
I have to push a button in the game that gives you a mandatory 24-hour warning

38:15.440 --> 38:17.240
so you can schedule your defense.

38:17.380 --> 38:18.660
Let's play that scenario out.

38:19.080 --> 38:20.400
Does it solve the sleep disruption?

38:20.820 --> 38:21.680
Yes, absolutely.

38:21.840 --> 38:22.660
The ambush is gone.

38:23.000 --> 38:24.860
But consider how it feels to play.

38:25.020 --> 38:25.600
It's boring.

38:26.000 --> 38:30.160
It severely diminishes the immediacy, the thrill, and the stakes of live play.

38:30.160 --> 38:37.160
It turns a dynamic, real-time war game into a slow-moving, bureaucratic calendar management simulation.

38:37.940 --> 38:40.400
You are scheduling conflicts like you schedule a dentist appointment.

38:40.520 --> 38:40.960
Exactly.

38:41.340 --> 38:47.020
It might entirely destroy the very psychological dynamic that makes the genre popular in the first place.

38:47.220 --> 38:48.820
What if we just make the losses reversible?

38:49.360 --> 38:53.700
You wake up, you log in, you see your base was destroyed by a midnight raid,

38:53.780 --> 38:58.480
but you just click an undo button, pay a tiny fee, and everything is rebuilt instantly.

38:58.480 --> 39:05.820
If losses are universally and easily reversible, the L variable in our exposure integral drops to nearly zero,

39:06.220 --> 39:08.020
which does solve the extraction problem.

39:08.200 --> 39:11.760
But, again, it fundamentally lowers the stakes of the game entirely.

39:11.940 --> 39:13.860
Why care about defending anything at all?

39:14.020 --> 39:14.340
Right.

39:14.660 --> 39:19.620
Why would an alliance spend weeks coordinating a massive defensive strategy

39:19.620 --> 39:22.720
if losing the territory means absolutely nothing?

39:22.840 --> 39:24.540
The persistence loses its meaning.

39:24.760 --> 39:26.940
So every mechanical fix seems to ruin the game.

39:26.940 --> 39:28.260
What about a social fix?

39:28.740 --> 39:30.600
What about mandatory role redundancy?

39:30.900 --> 39:32.360
Mandatory role redundancy.

39:32.580 --> 39:36.040
The game's code literally refuses to let you be the sole defense commander.

39:36.360 --> 39:40.020
It forces you to share the permission roles with three other people from different time zones.

39:40.260 --> 39:45.480
That is a fascinating approach because it directly attacks the U variable substitutability.

39:45.740 --> 39:47.680
It forces delegation through code.

39:47.680 --> 39:51.500
This is perhaps the most structurally sound mitigation discussed,

39:51.640 --> 39:56.780
but it still requires an incredibly high degree of social management from the players themselves.

39:57.000 --> 40:03.120
Yeah, to ensure those redundant players are actually competent, communicative, and awake during their shifts.

40:03.260 --> 40:04.540
There is no silver bullet.

40:05.260 --> 40:08.560
And when you realize how hard this is to fix through game design,

40:08.940 --> 40:13.420
you realize that the regulatory side of this is an absolute nightmare.

40:13.640 --> 40:14.220
Total nightmare.

40:14.220 --> 40:19.280
With a loot box, a regulator can point to a screen, look at a transaction, and say,

40:19.480 --> 40:20.620
that is a point of sale.

40:21.040 --> 40:23.580
You are charging money for a randomized chance.

40:24.060 --> 40:25.220
Regulate it like a casino.

40:25.600 --> 40:28.840
But temporal extraction is entirely invisible.

40:29.240 --> 40:35.820
Regulating structural unfinishability would require a massive, unprecedented paradigm shift in how we view consumer software.

40:36.260 --> 40:39.000
You wouldn't be regulating these applications like slot machines.

40:39.220 --> 40:42.020
You would have to start treating the users almost like laborers.

40:42.020 --> 40:43.400
As you know, tech company wants.

40:43.400 --> 40:49.780
You would be looking at occupational scheduling protections or drafting entirely new software design standards,

40:49.880 --> 40:55.660
specifically addressing circadian rhythms, especially given that these games legally target 13-year-olds.

40:55.940 --> 41:00.180
It asks lawmakers to govern the invisible social architecture of time,

41:00.400 --> 41:03.640
which is notoriously difficult to codify.

41:03.640 --> 41:07.340
Which brings us to the end of our journey today.

41:07.540 --> 41:09.400
And what a journey it has been.

41:10.180 --> 41:12.020
It is time to zoom out.

41:12.360 --> 41:14.900
Because as we wrap up, if you are listening to this,

41:15.000 --> 41:20.680
I know you are already thinking about how this architecture applies to the world far beyond the App Store.

41:20.680 --> 41:26.180
Because the core concept we've explored today, the topology of permissible disengagement,

41:26.440 --> 41:30.360
does not stop at the edge of a smartphone screen playing a war game.

41:30.440 --> 41:31.380
Oh, absolutely not.

41:31.540 --> 41:37.860
If unfinishability is fundamentally about systems that make your absence visible, consequential, and social costly,

41:38.420 --> 41:42.140
then we are increasingly surrounded by these architectures in our daily lives.

41:42.280 --> 41:43.800
Just think about your professional life.

41:43.960 --> 41:47.920
Think about getting a Slack or Teams message from your boss on a Sunday afternoon.

41:47.920 --> 41:50.980
Technically, legally, you don't have to answer it.

41:51.180 --> 41:52.580
But the little green dot is there.

41:52.680 --> 41:55.600
But the system shows a little green dot if you are near your phone.

41:55.700 --> 41:57.360
It shows that you are away if you aren't.

41:57.740 --> 41:59.080
The project timeline is ticking.

41:59.680 --> 42:04.460
And the social responsibility, the R variable to your coworkers, is incredibly high.

42:04.760 --> 42:08.700
The software structure makes your disengagement highly perilous to your career standing.

42:08.820 --> 42:13.260
Or consider the reality of gig economy workers, or on-call medical staff,

42:13.260 --> 42:17.380
or professionals tied to always-live global financial markets.

42:17.380 --> 42:24.860
The digital infrastructure we increasingly rely on for labor mirrors the exact architecture of these unfinishable games.

42:25.540 --> 42:28.680
It leverages our social guilt, our professional obligations,

42:29.000 --> 42:33.760
and our desire for standing within a group to extract continuous availability.

42:34.320 --> 42:39.360
It is flattening our natural circadian rhythms into a state of constant low-level vigilance.

42:39.500 --> 42:41.780
We started this conversation talking about boundaries.

42:42.500 --> 42:45.920
The physical act of walking off the basketball court knowing you are done.

42:45.920 --> 42:50.460
We've seen how a specific genre of software destroys that boundary entirely,

42:50.980 --> 42:55.240
separating the extraction of your money from the much more insidious extraction of your time.

42:55.360 --> 42:58.760
We need entirely different vocabularies to combat these different threats.

42:59.380 --> 43:03.580
You simply cannot fix a sleep deprivation engine by regulating it like a slot machine.

43:03.920 --> 43:09.660
And the researcher leaves us with a final, profound symmetry to consider as we navigate this digital future.

43:10.260 --> 43:13.660
In society, we often argue fiercely about the historical record.

43:13.660 --> 43:19.240
We argue that a digital system shouldn't be allowed to retroactively rewrite the facts of the past

43:19.240 --> 43:22.820
just because new information arises or because it's convenient.

43:23.100 --> 43:27.540
We defend the integrity of what has already happened against unbounded revision.

43:27.540 --> 43:33.200
But Flyxion points out that we must be equally, if not more, vigilant in defending our future.

43:33.200 --> 43:38.680
A persistent digital system, whether it's a mobile game, a corporate messaging app, or a social network,

43:39.120 --> 43:46.600
should not be allowed to use your standing membership in a group to claim unlimited, retroactive authority over your future availability.

43:46.600 --> 44:02.840
So as you look at your phone today, as you look at the mosaic of apps demanding your attention,

44:03.100 --> 44:07.540
the unread notifications piling up, the group chats waiting for your input,

44:07.540 --> 44:14.200
I want you to mull this over, what areas of your own life, what digital systems that you have willingly joined

44:14.200 --> 44:18.040
have quietly staked an unbounded claim on your future time?

44:18.440 --> 44:21.820
When was the last time you were truly able to step off the court, hear the buzzer sound,

44:21.920 --> 44:26.620
and know with absolute biological certainty that the game was actually over?

44:26.900 --> 44:29.260
Until next time, keep questioning the architecture around you.

44:29.320 --> 44:30.660
We'll see you on the next Deep Dive.

